menu "UDS bridge"

config UDS_LINK_PORT
    bool "Game Boy link port wired to GPIOs (no GB-Link)"
    default n
    help
        A GBA/GBC/Game Boy link port wired to this board through a level shifter, with no GB-Link: the board clocks the cartridge
        itself, as the GB-Link's Pico would. With no host on USB and the 3DS key stored, the board runs the Game Boy wrapper on its
        own; a host's HELLO takes the board back, and mGBA's uds-gblink-relay --port starts it with the log on the PC. The defaults
        are the shared VC-Adapter / GB-Link pinout on a Seeed XIAO ESP32-S3: SC on D0, SI on D1, SO on D2, SD on D3. SI and SO are
        named from this board's side, as on the GB-Link: this board's SI takes the console's SO, its SO feeds the console's SI. See
        the README's "GBA link port".

config UDS_LINK_PORT_SC_GPIO
    int "GPIO for SC, the clock (port pin 5)"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    default 1

config UDS_LINK_PORT_SI_GPIO
    int "GPIO for SI, this board's serial in (from the console's SO, port pin 2)"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    default 2

config UDS_LINK_PORT_SO_GPIO
    int "GPIO for SO, this board's serial out (to the console's SI, port pin 3)"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    default 3

config UDS_LINK_PORT_SD_GPIO
    int "GPIO for SD (port pin 4), -1 for none"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    range -1 48
    default 4
    help
        Wired for the shared pinout, unused in a Game Boy's link (only GBA multiplayer uses SD): the pin is held as an input with no
        pull, so it never drives the line.

config UDS_LINK_PORT_SENSE_GPIO
    int "ADC1 GPIO reading the port's VCC (pin 1) through a 1:2 divider, -1 for none"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    range -1 48
    default -1
    help
        Two equal resistors (100k each) from the port's VCC to GND, their middle to this pin (an ADC1 pin: GPIO1 to GPIO10 on the
        ESP32-S3; the XIAO's D4 is GPIO5). With it the board knows whether a console is on the port and in GB/GBC mode (5 V) or
        running a GBA game (3.3 V): it starts on its own only for a Game Boy, and stops when the console goes off. Without it the
        board assumes a Game Boy is there.

config UDS_LINK_PORT_CLOCK_HZ
    int "Link clock while a byte goes out, in Hz"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    range 8192 1000000
    default 262144
    help
        How fast the 8 bits of each byte are clocked (how often bytes go is the pacing below). 262144 is a Game Boy Color's fast serial
        clock, well inside what a Game Boy takes as a slave, and makes a byte about 31 us. The GB-Link's Pico clocks at about 1 MHz.
        8192, a Game Boy's own clock, makes a byte a millisecond: long enough that a cartridge re-arming its serial port in the
        middle of one loses its place (the first hardware run, firmware 1.8 before this setting).

config UDS_LINK_PORT_PACE_US
    int "Extra wait after each byte while roles are chosen and data is exchanged, in microseconds"
    depends on UDS_LINK_PORT
    range 0 20000
    default 2500
    help
        The gaps between bytes were measured with a GB-Link, where each byte also made a round trip through a PC (about 2.5 ms): the
        cartridge got about 3 ms between bytes, and trades worked. The link port has no round trip, so this stands in for it, keeping
        the gaps a cartridge has been seen to keep up with. The idle, sync and menu loops keep their 12.6 ms period either way.

endmenu
